<h2 id="qualifying">National Insurance and qualifying years</h2>

<p>Entitlement is built up through qualifying years. A qualifying year is a tax year in which a person paid, or was credited with, enough National Insurance.</p>

<h3 id="the-new-state-pension">The new State Pension</h3>
<p>One date apart from date of birth decides how the years count: when the person's National Insurance record began.</p>
<ul>
  <li>A record that began after April 2016 needs {{ m.f("nsp-full-rate-years") }} qualifying years for the full rate. {{ m.ref(("nsp-full-rate-years" | figure).source) }}</li>
  <li>A record that began earlier was given a starting amount: see the note below.</li>
  <li>Either way, each qualifying year added after <span class="date">5 April 2016</span> adds about {{ m.f("nsp-per-qualifying-year") }} a week, up to the full rate. {{ m.ref(("nsp-per-qualifying-year" | figure).source) }}</li>
</ul>

{% call m.note("35 years does not guarantee the full rate for everyone", 4) %}
<p>Most people retiring now have a record that began before April 2016. For them, the government worked out a starting amount from their record up to April 2016. {{ m.c("starting-amount") }}</p>
<p>The starting amount was reduced for any years the person was contracted out. Being contracted out meant paying lower National Insurance and building up a workplace or personal pension in place of the Additional State Pension. {{ m.c("contracted-out") }}</p>
<p>So a person with 35 or more years can be below the full rate, and a person with fewer than 35 can reach it.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h3 id="the-basic-state-pension">The basic State Pension</h3>
<p>The years needed for the full basic State Pension depend on date of birth and sex. They are in <a href="#which-system">the table of dates of birth</a>.</p>

<h3 id="national-insurance-credits">National Insurance credits</h3>
<p>People who are not paying National Insurance can get credits in some situations, for example when they are unemployed, ill, or caring for a child or an adult. Some credits are automatic. Others have to be claimed. {{ m.c("ni-credits") }}</p>

<h3 id="filling-gaps-in-a-record">Filling gaps in a record</h3>
<p>A person with gaps in their record may be able to pay voluntary National Insurance contributions to fill them. Paying does not always increase the pension, for example if the person was contracted out. GOV.UK says to check first: a person below State Pension age in the <a href="https://www.gov.uk/check-state-pension">State Pension forecast</a>, which says whether paying would add anything, and a person who has reached it with the Pension Service. {{ m.c("voluntary-not-always") }} The <a href="https://www.gov.uk/check-national-insurance-record">National Insurance record</a> shows the gaps, and the <a href="https://www.gov.uk/voluntary-national-insurance-contributions">guidance on voluntary contributions</a> says who can pay, what it costs and how to pay.</p>

<p>The rules for time spent abroad changed from <span class="date">6 April 2026</span>. For the tax year 2026 to 2027 onwards, Class 2 contributions, the lower weekly rate, cannot be paid for time abroad, and Class 3 contributions for time abroad after <span class="date">5 April 2026</span> need 10 years of living in the UK in a row, or 10 years of paid contributions, not counting credits. The old rules asked for 3 years, and still apply to applications made before 6 April 2026. {{ m.c("voluntary-abroad-2026") }}</p>

<h2 id="what-people-receive">On average, people received less than the full new rate then in force</h2>

<p>The average payment on the new State Pension was below the full new rate in force at the time, the average on the older system was lower still, and on the older system women received less than men. These are averages of the State Pension in payment in March 2026. They include any Additional State Pension, and any protected payment: an amount above the full new rate for a person whose starting amount in 2016 was higher. They are at the rates in force before the increase of April 2026, so they are not comparable with the 2026/27 full rates above.</p>

